## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an offline-first image pipeline with robust caching, memory management, and accessibility in a real UI scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Two-tier caching (memory + disk)
- LRU eviction under memory pressure
- Prefetching and cancellation tied to visible cells
- Accessibility: Dynamic Type and VoiceOver labels
- Async/await data loading and main-thread UI updates

## Code Example
```swift
import UIKit

actor ImageLoader {
    private let memCache = NSCache<NSString, UIImage>()
    private let fileManager = FileManager.default
    private let diskDir: URL

    init(diskFolder: URL) { self.diskDir = diskFolder }

    func load(from url: URL) async -> UIImage? {
        let key = url.absoluteString as NSString
        if let image = memCache.object(forKey: key) { return image }
        let path = diskDir.appendingPathComponent(url.lastPathComponent)
        if let data = try? Data(contentsOf: path), let image = UIImage(data: data) {
            memCache.setObject(image, forKey: key)
            return image
        }
        guard let (data, _) = try? await URLSession.shared.data(from: url),
              let image = UIImage(data: data) else { return nil }
        memCache.setObject(image, forKey: key)
        try? data.write(to: path)
        return image
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical iOS fundamentals: Codable data models, file-based persistence with atomic writes, and SwiftUI data flow. It also exercises UX concerns like debounce for responsiveness and accessibility considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Codable data model for Note with title, body, tags, and date
- File I/O in Documents with atomic write pattern
- SwiftUI state management using @Published and binding for filters
- Combine debouncing for search input
- Tag-based filtering logic and accessibility labeling

## Code Example
```javascript
import Foundation

struct Note: Codable, Identifiable {
  var id: UUID = UUID()
  var title: String
  var body: String
  var tags: [String]
  var date: Date = Date()
}

final class NotesStore: ObservableObject {
  @Published var notes: [Note] = []
  private let fileURL: URL = FileManager.default
    .urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
    .appendingPathComponent("notes.json")

  init() { load() }

  func load() {
    guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: fileURL) else { return }
    if let loaded = try? JSONDecoder().decode([Note].self, from: data) {
      notes = loaded
    }
  }

  func save() {
    let tempURL = fileURL.appendingPathExtension("tmp")
    guard let data = try? JSONEncoder().encode(notes) else { return }
    try? data.write(to: tempURL)
    try? FileManager.default.replaceItemAtURL(fileURL, withItemAtURL: tempURL)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to thousands of notes while keeping memory usage low?
- How would you test atomic write correctness and debounce behavior?
- How would you migrate to Core Data or add cloud sync later?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses offline-first design, delta-based syncing, and conflict handling under intermittent connectivity, which are critical in collaboration apps used by teams.

## Key Concepts

- Core Data data model for Comment and Patch objects
- Delta/patch format including baseVersion, changes, and timestamps
- Reconciliation strategy (latest-writer-wins or server-validated merges)
- Robust test plan for offline edits, re-connect, and concurrency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Swift-like pseudocode: apply a patch to a Comment
func apply(patch: Patch, to comment: inout Comment) {
    // Validate base version matches current state
    guard patch.baseVersion == comment.version else {
        // Handle conflict resolution
        resolveConflict(patch: patch, comment: &comment)
        return
    }

    // Apply delta changes
    for change in patch.changes {
        switch change.type {
        case .insert:
            comment.text.insert(change.text, at: change.position)
        case .delete:
            comment.text.removeSubrange(change.range)
        case .replace:
            comment.text.replaceSubrange(change.range, with: change.text)
        }
    }

    // Update version and timestamps
    comment.version += 1
    comment.updatedAt = Date()
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests offline-first caching, atomic disk writes, and SwiftUI grid rendering with a testable data layer.

## Key Concepts

- SwiftUI LazyVGrid for gallery
- URLSession data tasks for image fetch
- FileManager and atomic writes to Documents cache
- Codable parsing of bundled JSON
- Persistence across restarts and simple unit tests

## Code Example

```swift
import Foundation
import SwiftUI

struct ImageItem: Decodable { let id: String; let url: String }

class GalleryVM: ObservableObject {
    @Published var items: [ImageItem] = []
    let cacheDir: URL = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0].appendingPathComponent("imageCache")

    init() { loadMetadata() }

    func loadMetadata() {
        // load from Bundle JSON (omitted for brevity)
    }

    func localPath(for id: String) -> URL {
        cacheDir.appendingPathComponent("\(id).jpg")
    }

    func fetchIfNeeded(item: ImageItem) async {
        let path = localPath(for: item.id)
        if FileManager.default.fileExists(atPath: path.path) { return }
        guard let url = URL(string: item.url) else { return }
        let (data, _) = try! await URLSession.shared.data(from: url)
        // write atomically
        let tmp = path.appendingPathExtension("tmp")
        try! data.write(to: tmp)
        try! FileManager.default.moveItem(at: tmp, to: path)
        await MainActor.run { self.objectWillChange.send() }
    }
}
```

